WEBVTT - AICTE Security Deposit & FDR Portal (SIH PS 26265) Architecture Tour

00:00.000 --> 00:04.000
Hello everyone.

00:04.000 --> 00:11.000
In this video, I will explain the technical architecture of our AICTE Security Deposit and FDR Verification Portal.

00:11.000 --> 00:20.000
We will follow one request through the system to understand how our APIs, database, technologies, data pipelines and routing work together to create a secure and reliable workflow.

00:20.000 --> 00:24.000
First, let's understand the API architecture.

00:24.000 --> 00:31.000
Our frontend uses React and TypeScript, while our backend is built with FastAPI and Python.

00:31.000 --> 00:39.000
When a user submits an FDR, the request passes through the secure entry point and reaches the appropriate API endpoint.

00:39.000 --> 00:47.000
The backend checks the user's identity, validates the incoming data and verifies whether the requested action is allowed.

00:47.000 --> 00:57.000
We organize APIs by responsibility: instruments API, deposits API, bank API, release API, and audit API.

00:57.000 --> 01:04.000
A key feature is our server-driven workflow: the backend determines the next valid action based on state, permissions, and business rules.

01:04.000 --> 01:10.000
Backend idempotency protection ensures network retries never create duplicate deposits or repeat releases.

01:10.000 --> 01:15.000
Next, let's look at the database architecture built on PostgreSQL.

01:15.000 --> 01:22.000
Institutions connect to FDR records, and FDR records connect to verification logs and approval workflows.

01:22.000 --> 01:31.000
We enforce strict Row-Level Security: colleges only access their own records, regional officers see their jurisdiction, and head office views consolidated national reports.

01:31.000 --> 01:37.000
All financial amounts are stored as integer paise to prevent floating-point rounding errors.

01:37.000 --> 01:44.000
Role segregation is enforced at the database level: the user who prepares a submission can never approve or release it.

01:44.000 --> 01:55.000
Every action is recorded in a cryptographically linked append-only audit trail with SHA-256 hashes for total tamper detection.

01:55.000 --> 01:59.000
Now let's examine our technology choices and external connections.

01:59.000 --> 02:06.000
The frontend is built with React, Vite, and Tailwind CSS. The backend uses FastAPI, Python, SQLAlchemy, and Alembic.

02:06.000 --> 02:13.000
PostgreSQL handles data storage while Redis and Celery manage background tasks such as bank verification polling and reminders.

02:13.000 --> 02:22.000
For external systems, we integrate with Core Banking Solutions, the AICTE Institution Master Database, and notification services for email and SMS.

02:22.000 --> 02:35.000
Security layers protect every connection: JWT tokens, role-based access control, TLS encryption in transit, AES-256 at rest, and digital signature verification.

02:35.000 --> 02:40.000
Let's follow a deposit through its entire lifecycle across eight automated stages.

02:40.000 --> 02:48.000
Stage 1: The college submits deposit details. Stage 2: The system validates bank IFSC, amount against AICTE regulation tables, and required formats.

02:48.000 --> 02:56.000
Stage 3: An automated verification request is sent to the bank. Stage 4: The bank confirms lien marked in favor of AICTE, sealing the tripartite agreement.

02:56.000 --> 03:05.000
Stage 5: The FDR enters active monitoring with interest tracking. Stage 6: Automated maturity alerts trigger at T-90, T-60, and T-30 days.

03:05.000 --> 03:14.000
Stage 7: The college applies for release and uploads necessary compliance certificates.

03:14.000 --> 03:24.000
Stage 8: Regional officers review, Finance Bureau validates, and an automated release order is issued with digital signature verification.

03:24.000 --> 03:40.000
Every state transition is locked, audited, and mathematically verified without manual paper chasing.

03:40.000 --> 03:45.000
Next, let's look at production network routing and interface structure.

03:45.000 --> 03:53.000
Client requests enter through Caddy reverse proxy, providing automatic HTTPS, compression, and rate limiting.

03:53.000 --> 04:01.000
Requests for the API route to the FastAPI backend, while other requests serve the static React single-page application.

04:01.000 --> 04:10.000
Our frontend architecture uses modular route-based structures with TanStack Router, responsive layouts, and accessible components.

04:10.000 --> 04:20.000
Real-time updates keep dashboards synchronized across Colleges, Bank Officers, Regional Officers, and National Administrators.

04:20.000 --> 04:25.000
To summarize, our architecture provides four core advantages:

04:25.000 --> 04:31.000
First, Scalability: Stateless APIs and asynchronous task processing handle peak workloads during annual approval cycles.

04:31.000 --> 04:37.000
Second, Security: Row-level security, role separation, and cryptographic audit trails protect sensitive financial data.

04:37.000 --> 04:43.000
Third, Reliability: Idempotent operations, automated retries, and comprehensive error handling prevent data loss.

04:43.000 --> 04:50.000
Fourth, Regulatory Compliance: Strict enforcement of AICTE norms with automated validation and complete auditability. Thank you.
